  "query_preview": "..\n\nA couple of thunderstorm clusters have developed over western Lower \nMichigan this afternoon along a continuous line of agitated cumulus \nthat stretches from northern MI back toward central Illinois. The \ntrend so far has been for cells to be short-lived as they move off \nthe boundary and away from the instability axis, which is anchored \nclosely to the cold front itself. Local cloud trends are quite \nstable still, reflecting remnants of the 850-600mb stable layer that \nwas sampled in the 12z KDTX sounding. \n\nExpectations for the afternoon-evening are still largely on track \nwhere coverage increases to become a broken line of showers and \nstorms. Any deeper convective growth is likely confined to a window \nof about 4pm-9pm as moisture convergence sweeps through ahead of the \nfront and overlaps with weakening shear profiles. The lack of deep \nlayer shear favors a pulsey/multicellular nature rather than strong \ncold pool organization, meaning isolated downburst and hail \npotential as cores collapse. Given the environment, higher \nconfidence in a strong-severe storm exists near the Ohio border \nwhere instability is greatest although all of SE MI is in at least a \nMarginal Risk for severe weather. Veered south/southwest flow is \ngenerally less favorable for the streamwise vorticity needed for \ntornadoes with a west-east mean wind. That said, there has been weak \nmid-level rotation noted upstream so cannot rule out supercellular \nstructures, mainly early on as shear is at its peak. \n\nThe cold front sweeps through late this evening, bringing an end to \nthunderstorm chances overnight. A quieter pattern then develops \nThursday into the weekend as surface high pressure fills in post \ncold front. Northwest flow and return of thermal troughing take \nadvantage of warmer lake temperatures to generate partial coverage \nof stratocumulus Thursday, which could squeeze out low-topped shower \nactivity. Moisture availability then wanes Friday into the weekend \nas the high drifts overhead, with winds then shifting to the \nsouthwest by Saturday in response. Dry conditions thus expected. \nCoolest high temperatures of the period expected Thursday-Friday as \nh8 temperatures drop into the lower teens across Lower MI, equating \nto highs in the upper 70s and lows in the 50s. \n\nThe emergence of SW flow Saturday then kicks off a warming trend \nthat peaks early next week. GFS and Euro MOS guidance are in line \nwith the extended NBM, projecting high temperatures near 90 degrees \nMonday-Tuesday. This pairs with a gulf connection where moisture \ntransport is accelerated Sunday through Tuesday as low level jet \nwinds increase to 30-35 knots. This establishes breezy southwest \nflow (~25 to 30 mph) during peak heating each day, as well as an \nincrease in shower and thunderstorm chances across the Great Lakes.",
